.TH COMPID 1 "2026-10-20" "compid" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
compid \- fingerprint compiled binaries and predict how they were built
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B compid
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.RB [ \-\-help ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ args ]
.PP
One command per line; brackets mark optional arguments.
.PP
.B compid extract
.I BIN
.RB [ \-o
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B compid classify
.I BIN
.RB [ \-\-model
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-confidence\-threshold
.IR P ]
.br
.B compid explain
.I BIN
.RB [ \-\-model
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B compid compare
.I A
.I B
.br
.B compid model\-info
.RB [ \-\-model
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B compid dataset\-info
.RB [ \-\-manifest
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B compid batch
.I MANIFEST
.RB [ \-o
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B compid functions
.I BIN
.RB [ \-\-model
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-features
.IR FILE ]
.br
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-measure ]
.br
.B compid functions\-batch
.I MANIFEST
.RB [ \-o
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-features ]
.br
.B compid completions
.RB { bash | zsh | fish | powershell }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B compid
answers, for a given compiled binary: which compiler and version built it,
at what optimization level, for which architecture and OS, against which
libc, and whether it is PIE, LTO-flavored, statically linked, or built with
hardening flags. It works on ELF, PE (Windows, x86-64), and Mach-O (macOS,
x86-64 and arm64) binaries; the container is detected from the file's magic
bytes, and none of the answers require symbols or debug info.
.PP
Predictions come from a small linear model over integer features counted in
the executable section (opcode and byte n-gram shape, section layout, import
tables, dynamic-loader names). The training pipeline produces the model; the
extractor also works standalone to dump raw features. Output for a given
(binary, model, feature schema) triple is deterministic, so outputs are
diffable.
.PP
Defaults that matter: \-\-model defaults to
.I model/model.json
(\-\-model on
.B functions
defaults to
.IR model/functions.json ),
and \-\-manifest on
.B dataset\-info
defaults to
.IR dataset/manifest.csv .
All three resolve relative to the current directory; pass them explicitly
when running from elsewhere.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS compid extract
.B compid extract
.I BIN
.RB [ \-o
.IR FILE ]
.PP
Prints the feature JSON for one binary to stdout, or writes it to
.I FILE
with
.BR \-o .
The JSON follows the schema in docs/dataset.md:
.B build_id
and
.B labels
are empty strings for binaries outside the corpus, and
.B features
holds the integer counts over the executable section. PE output carries
os=windows, the architecture spelled from the COFF machine, and the
manifest's windows-row conventions for fields a PE does not express
(pie/static = n/a); Mach-O output carries os=macos, the architecture
spelled from the header cputype, pie=pie, and the dylib load-command names
as lowercase basename cmt: features. 32-bit Mach-O images are rejected with
a clear error.
.SS compid classify
.B compid classify
.I BIN
.RB [ \-\-model
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-confidence\-threshold
.IR P ]
.PP
Loads the model, extracts features from
.IR BIN ,
and prints, for each label field the model knows, up to three classes with
their probabilities. With
.BR \-\-json ,
the table becomes a machine-readable object: one entry per field, each a
map of class name to probability. Consumers should ignore unknown fields;
the schemas version themselves (format, feature_version).
.PP
.B \-\-confidence\-threshold
.I P
(default 0.6) flags any field whose top prediction falls below that
probability: the table prints a [low confidence] marker next to the field
name and the JSON output gains "low_confidence": true on the field. Pass 0
to disable flagging entirely. The flag is an abstention hint: a field below
the threshold is one where the honest answer is that the model is not sure,
and the top-3 listing is the real answer.
.SS compid explain
.B compid explain
.I BIN
.RB [ \-\-model
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.PP
For the top predicted class of each field, lists the features that
contributed most to that score (weight times normalized tf), plus the
notable contributions pulling toward the runner-up. Plainly human-readable
on purpose; the JSON form of the same data is
.B compid extract
plus the model.
.SS compid compare
.B compid compare
.I A
.I B
.PP
Prints the cosine similarity between the L2-normalized feature vectors of
binaries
.I A
and
.IR B .
1.0 means identical fingerprints (comparing a file with itself is the
trivially correct case); values near 0 mean unrelated builds. Useful
against a reference build you trust: a sharp drop in similarity after a
toolchain bump is exactly the kind of change this catches.
.SS compid model\-info
.B compid model\-info
.RB [ \-\-model
.IR FILE ]
.PP
Describe a trained model: format, the header line classify uses, every
field with its classes, and the provenance block (manifest row count and
sha256, feature row count and sha256) when the trainer recorded one.
.SS compid dataset\-info
.B compid dataset\-info
.RB [ \-\-manifest
.IR FILE ]
.PP
Reads the manifest CSV and prints, per label field, the count of rows for
each label value, along with the total row count and how many duplicate
SHA-256 rows were found. This is the quickest way to see what a dataset
actually contains before trusting anything trained on it.
.SS compid batch
.B compid batch
.I MANIFEST
.RB [ \-o
.IR FILE ]
.PP
Runs extraction for every manifest row whose binary exists. Feature objects
are emitted as JSON lines, one per binary, to stdout, or appended to
.I FILE
with
.BR \-o .
Rows whose binary is missing are skipped and summarized in a final line, so
a partial local checkout still processes everything present.
.SS compid functions
.B compid functions
.I BIN
.RB [ \-\-model
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-features
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-measure ]
.PP
Per-function provenance for one binary. Function boundaries come from the
ELF symbol table, so the input should be an unstripped ELF (a relocatable
object file works too). The table lists each function's raw (never
demangled) symbol name, its address as the symbol table records it, its
size, and, when a function-level model is loaded, the predicted
compiler_family and opt_level with per-function confidence, plus a footer
tallying the predicted families.
.PP
A stripped x86-64 ELF takes the fallback path: a conservative prologue scan
over the executable sections proposes the function starts (see
docs/FUNCTIONS-EVALUATION.md for the measured precision and recall), each
start is named func_<hex addr>, and the table carries a header note, so
synthesized names are never mistaken for symbol names. The
.B \-\-json
output marks the source explicitly ("source": "heuristic" versus
"symbol_table"). A stripped non-x86-64 ELF, a PE, and a Mach-O still print
their explicit reasons instead of a table and exit 0.
.B \-\-model
defaults to
.I model/functions.json
when that file exists; an explicitly passed
.B \-\-model
that does not exist is an error. A model file carries only the two
per-function fields, so the table never predicts container-level labels.
.PP
.B \-\-features
.I FILE
writes the per-function training JSONL instead of the table: one object per
function with build_id, name, addr (hex string), size, labels (the
extractor's own best-effort container labels), and features.
.B \-\-json
swaps the table for a machine-readable object with per-function
predictions, the boundary source, and the family tally.
.PP
.B \-\-measure
runs the honest self-check instead of the table: on an unstripped binary it
re-runs the prologue scan and scores the heuristic's starts against the
symbol table's starts at a 8-byte tolerance, printing counts plus
precision, recall, and F1
.RB ( \-\-json
for the machine-readable form). This is the command behind the measured
numbers in docs/FUNCTIONS-EVALUATION.md;
scripts/measure_boundaries.py sweeps it over a manifest sample. A stripped
binary has no ground truth to measure against and errors out.
.SS compid functions\-batch
.B compid functions\-batch
.I MANIFEST
.RB [ \-o
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-features ]
.PP
Runs per-function extraction over every manifest row whose binary exists
and carries functions: unstripped ELF via the symbol table, stripped
x86-64 ELF via the prologue heuristic. With
.BR \-\-features ,
writes the per-function training JSONL, one object per function with
build_id, name, addr, size, labels (the manifest's ground-truth labels for
that row), and features, which is the input training/train_functions.py
expects. Without
.BR \-\-features ,
writes a plain per-binary listing (name, address, size). Rows whose binary
is missing, yields no functions, or fails to parse are skipped with a
per-row stderr note and summarized in the final line.
.SS compid completions
.B compid completions
.RB { bash | zsh | fish | powershell }
.PP
Prints a shell completion script to stdout for one of bash, zsh, fish, or
powershell; no flags, just the script. The subcommand is hidden from
\-\-help because it is a one-time install helper, not a daily driver.
Install the script where your shell looks for completions:
.TP
bash
.B compid completions bash
> ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/compid (create the directory
first if it does not exist; the completion loads on the next interactive
shell)
.TP
zsh
.B compid completions zsh
> ~/.zfunc/_compid and make sure ~/.zfunc is in your fpath before compinit
runs
.TP
fish
.B compid completions fish
> ~/.config/fish/completions/compid.fish
.TP
powershell
.B compid completions powershell
>> $PROFILE (or dot-source the output from your profile)
.PP
The generated script covers the subcommands and flags of the build that
produced it, so regenerate it after upgrading compid. Unknown shell names
are rejected with a usage error (exit code 2) listing the supported ones.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success
.TP
.B 1
runtime error (unreadable binary, missing model, and the like)
.TP
.B 2
usage error (unknown flag, missing argument)
.SH SEE ALSO
Project documentation ships in the repository and in every release tarball
under docs/: cli.md (full CLI reference), architecture.md, dataset.md,
model-format.md, FUNCTIONS-EVALUATION.md, and EVALUATION.md.
.PP
.BR strip (1),
.BR file (1)
